PUBLIC RESPONSE: The French text that is quoted in this form is the post-visit break-up letter of the young Romanian reader of Sein und Zeit, Emil – Mihai-Emanoil – Cioran, written after his stay in Freiburg in the autumn of 1937 and sent in the winter of 1937-38.

It is the letter in which Cioran reports his disillusionment after having actually met Heidegger and which is habitually reproduced in the Cioran-Heidegger epistolary tradition:

* *“I believed that a man who can write Being and Time could understand something about men”*;
* the complaint that metaphysics has “enclosed a spirit within itself, confusing intimacy with hermeticism”;
* *“After all this, I leave as sad and tormented as I arrived, with the hidden pain of having been mistaken at a decisive moment in my life… I am not leaving angry… when something fades and leaves us empty, the fault lies within us, for we believed too quickly in a reality that did not exist”*;
* *“ultimate solitude is the only way to be close to others… Allow me to dispel any possible misunderstanding regarding this letter by calling you a friend. You will remain a friend to me in the secret solitude of my existence… And if you ever remember me, forget this episode (it is nothing more) and always believe in my exceptional friendship”*;
* and the closing aphorism *“the worth of an existence is not measured by the height of its achievements, but by the depth of its roots.”*

That is the French-language 1937/38 Cioran letter that Cioran himself recalls as the never-erased encounter with Heidegger. It is the text that is continually reprinted as Cioran’s 1937-38 letter to Heidegger.

The post-war Todtnauberg invitation to Jean Beaufret, Heidegger’s 13 July 1945 reply to Beaufret’s 8/13 July 1945 letter and the 1946-47 Dialogues, is a historically distinct episode. The Beaufret/Sartre 1945-48 reception is the source of the widespread secondary conflation in the literature, with Cioran’s 1937 wording being repeatedly reproduced under the names of Beaufret, Jean Wahl and, in popular accounts, Jean-Paul Sartre. Textually the passage you quote is the Cioran letter.

