PUBLIC RESPONSE: The text you quote is the French break-up / farewell letter that Mihai-Emanoil Cioran, Emil Cioran, wrote to Martin Heidegger after his personal encounter in Freiburg in the autumn of 1937.

It is the letter Cioran wrote in French after he had come to Freiburg to hear Heidegger, had actually seen and spoken with him and left deeply disillusioned. The whole vocabulary belongs to that 1937-38 Cioran-Heidegger epistolary episode:

* “I believed that a man who can write Being and Time could understand something about men”;
* the accusation that “metaphysics enclosed a spirit within itself, confusing intimacy with hermeticism” and that “intimacy offers a more delicate possibility of engaging with others without losing oneself in them”;
* “After all this, I leave as sad and tormented as I arrived, with the hidden pain of having been mistaken at a decisive moment in my life. But believe me, I am not leaving angry”;
* “when something fades and leaves us empty, the fault lies within us, for we believed too quickly in a reality that did not exist”;
* “ultimate solitude is the only way to be close to others… something has passed between the two of us that will never be erased from my being”;
* “Allow me to dispel any possible misunderstanding regarding this letter by calling you a friend. You will remain a friend to me in the secret solitude of my existence… And if you ever remember me, forget this episode (it is nothing more) and always believe in my exceptional friendship”,
* and the closing aphorism “the worth of an existence is not measured by the height of its achievements, but by the depth of its roots”.

That is the canonical Cioran letter, habitually reproduced in French and usually dated to the 1937 Freiburg visit and the correspondence that followed it. It is the letter Cioran recalls in his autobiographical writings as his painful, never erased encounter with Heidegger.

Heidegger did answer Cioran and, in the tone of the time, invited him to come and see him again at home in Freiburg and discussed a possible further visit, the exchange that is remembered as the painful post-visit reconciliation attempt between the young Romanian and Heidegger.

The passage has been widely re-circulated in the secondary literature on the post-war French reception of Heidegger and is commonly conflated with the Sartre/Wahl prototype and with Jean Beaufret’s 8/13 July 1945 and 1946 letters which actually opened the Todtnauberg dialogue. The invitation-to-the-Hütte motif is most famously associated with Beaufret’s 1945-46 correspondence, which is why the Cioran text is so often mis-attributed to Beaufret/Sartre. The wording itself, however, is Cioran’s 1937-38 French letter.

