It is the letter in which the young Romanian, who had come to Freiburg in order to hear Heidegger and who had already written to him about Sein und Zeit, reports his disillusionment after the actual meeting. 

* “Je croyais qu’un homme capable d’écrire Être et Temps pouvait comprendre quelque chose aux hommes” – “I believed that a man who can write Being and Time could understand something about men”;
* the reproach that “metaphysics enclosed a spirit within itself, confusing intimacy with hermeticism”;
* “Après tout cela, je pars aussi triste et tourmenté que je suis arrivé, avec la douleur cachée de m’être trompé à un moment décisif de ma vie”;
* the insistence that he is not leaving angry, “quand quelque chose s’éteint et nous laisse vides, la faute est en nous, car nous avons cru trop vite à une réalité qui n’existait pas”;
* “l’ultime solitude est la seule façon d’être proche des autres”, “Permettez-moi de dissiper tout malentendu éventuel à propos de cette lettre en vous appelant ami. Vous resterez un ami pour moi dans la solitude secrète de mon existence… Et si vous pensez jamais à moi, oubliez cet épisode… et croyez toujours à mon amitié exceptionnelle”; 
* the closing aphorism “la valeur d’une existence ne se mesure pas à la hauteur de ses réalisations mais à la profondeur de ses racines”, “the worth of an existence is not measured by the height of its achievements, but by the depth of its roots”.

That is the classic 1937-38 French disillusionment letter of Cioran, regularly quoted as the post-visit letter in which he says something has passed between them that will never be erased from his being. It is the letter that elicited Heidegger’s kindly, conciliatory reply in which Heidegger tries to keep the contact alive and invites Cioran to come and see him again at home, first in Freiburg and in the discussion of a possible visit to Todtnauberg.

The wording is the one that secondary literature often misplaces in the post-war French reception and consequently mixes up with the Sartre / Beaufret 1945-48 correspondence. The canonical Todtnauberg invitation of 13 July 1945 is indeed the Beaufret invitation, which is why the two traditions are so often conflated. Textually and in the contemporary correspondence the French farewell quoted here is Cioran’s 1937-38 letter to Heidegger.

